# Remote Qwen endpoint, run as an environment on the shared layer.
#
# This file is hand-maintained — nothing generates or rewrites it. The shared
# infrastructure is deployed once per account with `outfit remote bootstrap`;
# then, from this directory:
#   outfit remote deploy  # create the environment REMOTE names + set what it
#                         # serves (this file + preset); registers its
#                         # remote.json under ~/.config/outfit/remotes/
#   outfit remote start   # boot (or re-wake a stopped) the instance; prints
#                         # the OPENAI_API_KEY export
#   outfit apply          # point your coding agent at the endpoint
#   outfit remote pause   # stop now (kept for a re-wake) instead of waiting
#                         # for the idle timer
#   outfit remote stop    # terminate now instead of waiting for the idle timer
#
# PROVIDER names the inference engine, so this file is what chooses llama.cpp
# over vLLM: `outfit remote deploy` asks the cloud for that engine, and the same
# file runs it locally under `outfit serve`. To cut back to vLLM, set PROVIDER
# to vllm, drop PRESET, and set MODEL to the FP8 repo (Qwen/Qwen3.6-27B-FP8).
# Either way the deploy Lambda seeds the weights into S3 if they are not there.
#
# ALIAS is both the name your coding agent asks for and the name the server is
# started under (llama-server's --alias), so the two cannot drift apart.
#
# REMOTE names the environment: its EIP, API key and config are its own, so
# several Outfits can run several instances side by side in one account.
#
# There is deliberately no BASEURL: the environment's address belongs to the
# deployment, so `outfit apply` takes it from the environment's remote.json.
# Add a BASEURL here only to override that.
PROVIDER llamacpp
ALIAS    qwen3.6-27b
CONTEXT  131072
PRESET   ./preset.ini
REMOTE   qwen3.6-27b
